is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the campus DNS resolver on SRV1 (10.5.0.4). Clients in Office A (e.g., PC1 at 10.1.0.100) all point to 10.5.0.4 as their only nameserver. SRV1’s BIND configuration explicitly has “recursion no;” in /etc/bind/named.conf, and client queries to external domains are refused with “status: REFUSED; WARNING: recursion requested but not available” (verified from PC1/PC2/PC3 via dig @10.5.0.4). This causes browsers and apps to intermittently fall back to alternate mechanisms (e.g., cached IPs, DoH), producing “slow and intermittent” Internet symptom even though transport is fine.

Mechanism: DNS lookups for Internet domains fail at the local resolver, adding delay while applications retry/fallback; some cached lookups succeed, making behavior seem sporadic.

Localization: SRV1 eth0 (10.5.0.4) running named; recursion disabled. LAN and WAN paths are healthy: PC1 reaches R1 and both ISPs with low RTT and 0% loss; R1 NAT is configured and default route via 203.0.113.1 is up.

Root cause: SRV1’s BIND is misconfigured as an authoritative-only server with recursion disabled while clients rely on it for Internet resolution.

Corrective fix: On SRV1, enable recursion (and optionally forwarders) in BIND, then restart named. For example, in /etc/bind/named.conf set “recursion yes;” (and in named.conf.options add forwarders { 8.8.8.8; 1.1.1.1; }; if desired). Then restart the DNS daemon (service named or bind9, depending on unit) on SRV1. No WAN link or R1 failover is needed.
=== END ===